# SYNTAX TEST "text.html.tiddlywiki5" "An argument name answers to its surface"

# A call writes a named argument on a TEMPLATE surface, the way html, Vue, JSX, Liquid,
# Handlebars and MediaWiki's own `{{T|name=v}}` do, so it takes `entity.other.attribute-name`
# and reads as a widget attribute reads. Measured: under the argument's own family a call's
# name and its argument told apart in 25 of 65 bundled themes — in gruvbox-dark-medium both
# sat at #83a598 — and under the surface's family, 57.
#
# `variable.parameter` stands FIRST and names the declaration side a reader may still rule on.
# The name a reader SEES stands LAST: a space-separated name pushes its scopes in order and the
# innermost one wins.
#
# EACH SURFACE KEEPS ITS OWN TAIL, so a rule reaches a call's argument alone or a widget's
# attribute alone, and a rule on `entity.other.attribute-name` reaches both.
#
# A CALL'S ARGUMENT KEY ALSO CARRIES `support.type.property-name.argument`, appended last, so it
# parts from the called name where a theme paints `entity.name.function` and
# `entity.other.attribute-name` alike — gruvbox paints both #fabd2f. The published attribute name
# still stands for every theme ruling on it; the appended name decides only where a theme rules on
# the property-name family. A widget's attribute does not take it: the widget-against-call floor
# stands re-seated on the one-namespace ruling, and the attribute's family is what holds it there.
#
# THE SEPARATORS PART CORRECTLY, and the host says why. `parseutils.js#parseMacroParameterAsAttribute`
# records which of `=` and `:` an author wrote in `node.assignmentOperator`, then opens the
# filtered, indirect, macro, MVV and substituted value grammars only when it reads `=`. A widget
# attribute's `=` selects no such thing — `parseAttribute` offers every value form either way —
# so a call's `=` keeps `keyword.operator.assignment` and a widget's keeps
# `punctuation.separator.key-value`.
#
# THE DECLARATION SIDE STANDS CONTROLLED BY THE PINNED SNAPSHOTS. A pragma opens only in the
# pragma zone, which no specimen here can reach; `tests/samples/tiddlywiki5.pragma.tw.snap` pins
# `\define myMacro1(param:test, ...)` and holds every declared `param` at `variable.parameter`
# alone. TextMate 1.x seats that name "when the variable is declared as the parameter", which is
# that line and not a call.

! A call's argument reads as an attribute name

<<myproc param:"v" other="w">>
#        ^^^^^ entity.other.attribute-name.argument.tiddlywiki5
#        ^^^^^ variable.parameter.tiddlywiki5
#        ^^^^^ - entity.other.attribute-name.html.tiddlywiki5
#        ^^^^^ support.type.property-name.argument.tiddlywiki5
#                  ^^^^^ entity.other.attribute-name.argument.tiddlywiki5
#                  ^^^^^ support.type.property-name.argument.tiddlywiki5
#             ^ keyword.operator.assignment.tiddlywiki5
#                       ^ keyword.operator.assignment.tiddlywiki5
# ^^^^^^ variable.name.macro.tiddlywiki5
# ^^^^^^ - entity.other.attribute-name.argument.tiddlywiki5

! A widget's attribute keeps its own tail and its own separator

<$link to="x">go</$link>
#      ^^ entity.other.attribute-name.html.tiddlywiki5
#      ^^ - entity.other.attribute-name.argument.tiddlywiki5
#      ^^ - support.type.property-name.argument.tiddlywiki5
#        ^ punctuation.separator.key-value.html.tiddlywiki5
#        ^ - keyword.operator.assignment.tiddlywiki5

